WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.320
In 2018, a cockroach crawled
inside a sleeping man's ear

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.470
and laid an egg sac.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.150
Luckily, roaches don't go
out of their way to do this,

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.481
so it doesn't happen very often.

00:00:13.481 --> 00:00:16.163
But there are a bunch
of other unsavory bugs

00:00:16.163 --> 00:00:19.963
that will lay eggs all
over your body on purpose.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.300
First up, the human botfly.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.270
These insects, which are native to Central

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.270
and South America, glue
their eggs to mosquitoes

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.280
and other blood-sucking insects.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.170
When the mosquito bites
you, the eggs hatch.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.680
Then the larvae wriggle into your skin,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.910
creating a painful pimple that leaks pus.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.491
Or, as the baby botflies call it, lunch.

00:00:42.491 --> 00:00:44.950
After five to 10 weeks, they escape.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.350
Not long after that, they reach adulthood,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.864
ready to mate, and start
the cycle all over.

00:00:50.864 --> 00:00:53.940
Another tropical parasite
is Tunga penetrans,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.743
more commonly known as the sand flea.

00:00:56.743 --> 00:00:58.983
Females burrow into
the bottom of your foot

00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:00.750
and slurp your blood.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.150
They start off smaller
than a grain of sand

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.490
but grow to 2,000 times
their size within a week

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.750
as they swell with your
blood and up to 200 eggs.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.428
Those eggs fall to the ground and hatch,

00:01:13.428 --> 00:01:16.170
waiting for the next bare foot to pass by.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.350
But some egg-laying parasites
go more than just skin deep.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.188
Tapeworms, for example,
invade your intestines.

00:01:24.188 --> 00:01:26.600
Adults can grow to be
longer than a bowling lane

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.930
and block up your digestive system.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.443
But it gets worse,

00:01:30.443 --> 00:01:33.535
because they lay tens
of thousands of eggs,

00:01:33.535 --> 00:01:35.343
which can hatch and migrate,

00:01:35.343 --> 00:01:36.930
spreading throughout your lungs,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.680
muscles, and even your brain.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.130
If that sounds gruesome,
just wait till you hear

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.350
about the Loa loa worm.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.273
It can be passed from human to human

00:01:46.273 --> 00:01:49.500
by hitching a ride inside of deer flies.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.430
When the flies bite you, the
larvae enter through the wound.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.550
After five months growing
beneath your skin,

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.683
they reach adulthood and start to release

00:01:57.683 --> 00:02:00.880
thousands of embryos a day.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.135
Sometimes you can even see the worms

00:02:03.135 --> 00:02:07.378
moving under your skin
or across your eyeball.

00:02:07.378 --> 00:02:11.167
But, hey, not every egg-laying
invader is sinister.

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:13.560
Face mites, for example,
are pretty innocuous.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.580
They live on pretty much everyone's face,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.040
and most people just never notice.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.030
After all, they feed on
facial oil, not flesh.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.110
When it comes time to breed,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.220
females just lay one egg
inside of your pores.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.389
Even better, researchers can
now study your face mites

00:02:28.389 --> 00:02:32.125
to track how your ancestors
migrated across the planet,

00:02:32.125 --> 00:02:34.600
because we usually pass
them from parent to child,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.180
so the mites stay in the family.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.311
But face mites aren't the
only helpful bug around.

00:02:39.311 --> 00:02:42.280
Green bottle flies might
be useful in medicine.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.950
They lay their eggs inside of open wounds,

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.030
and then the maggots hatch
and devour the damaged flesh.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.520
That sounds brutal, but one day

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.530
we might be able to harness this process

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.280
to treat diabetic foot ulcers

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.430
and other slow-healing wounds.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.000
Because when the maggots go to town,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.270
they actually clean the area

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:01.270
and remove a lot of the dead tissue.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.408
They even secrete proteins
that reduce inflammation.

00:03:04.408 --> 00:03:06.743
So maybe botflies, fleas, and tapeworms

00:03:06.743 --> 00:03:08.764
could learn a thing or two

00:03:08.764 --> 00:03:10.280
and at least make themselves useful

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.980
if they're going to move right in.

